Dependency aliases rename a bound symbol for local use inside one file. They are distinct from type aliases and from public module re-export names.

### Definition and syntax

A clause-level namespace alias changes the local name of a bound namespace:

[codeblock, $code("moth"):
    @core/math as math
]

An entry alias changes the local name of one bound symbol:

[codeblock, $code("moth"):
    @core/math sin as sine, PI as CIRCLE_PI
]

### Exact contract

- A clause-level namespace alias changes the namespace name visible in one file.
- An entry alias changes one bound symbol visible in one file.
- The source declaration keeps its canonical exported identity.
- Ordinary dependency aliases are file-local.
- Ordinary dependency aliases are not automatically re-exported.
- Aliases work for every bindable symbol category the dependency surface supports: functions, constants, structs, choices, type aliases and external package members.
- External package aliases follow the same local collision policy as source dependency clauses.
- Each selected entry is renamed separately.
- A clause-level `as` and direct selections are mutually exclusive. There is no filtered namespace form.

### Collision rules

Dependency aliases must not collide with any visible name in the same file, including:

- same-file declarations
- other dependency clauses
- other aliases
- type aliases
- builtins
- preluded names

Aliases do not shadow an existing visible name. A collision is a compile error.

[codeblock, $code("moth"):
    render || -> String:
        return "local"
    ;

    -- Error: render is already visible in this file
    @other render
]

Resolve the collision by renaming the dependency.

[codeblock, $code("moth"):
    @other render as render_other
]

### Leading-case warnings

An alias whose leading case differs from the bound symbol's leading case produces a warning. The warning is intentionally simple: it catches obvious type-versus-value naming drift without trying to classify every naming convention.

[codeblock, $code("moth"):
    -- Warning
    @core/math sin as Sine

    -- Warning
    @core/math PI as pi
]

### Namespace aliases

Use a clause-level namespace alias when the bound path would be awkward to repeat or when two bound namespaces share a basename.

[codeblock, $code("moth"):
    @core/math as math
    @core/text as text

    value = math.sin(1.0)
    length = text.length("hello")
]

### Entry aliases

Use entry aliases when two packages expose the same name.

[codeblock, $code("moth"):
    @blog render as render_blog
    @docs render as render_docs

    blog_html = render_blog()
    docs_html = render_docs()
]

A clause cannot use a single alias for a direct-selection list. Clause-level `as` and direct selections are mutually exclusive.

[codeblock, $code("moth"):
    -- Error
    @components as Ui Button, Card

    -- Valid namespace alias
    @components as Ui
]

### Local and public-name distinction

An ordinary dependency alias is private to the file that declares it. A public name chosen in a module root's export surface is a different operation. Cross-module visibility is controlled by the module root's explicit `export:` surface. The normal module-root filename itself has no semantic role. The current export syntax is owned by @../project-structure (Project Structure) and @../packages (Packages). Do not treat a local dependency alias as a public API rename.

[codeblock, $code("moth"):
    -- Ordinary local dependency alias
    @models User as ModelUser
]

The local `ModelUser` is visible only in this file. A public rename belongs inside the module root's current explicit `export:` block. See @../project-structure (Project Structure) and @../packages (Packages) for the exact current form.

### Dependency aliases with type aliases

A type alias may target a bound type alias.

[codeblock, $code("moth"):
    @models User as ExternalUser

    CurrentUser as ExternalUser
]

This combines a file-local dependency rename with a domain-specific type alias.

### Related concepts

- For type aliases see the previous section.
- For payload capture aliases see the next section.
- For dependency syntax and module roots see @../packages (Packages) and @../project-structure (Project Structure).
